-- chem/dyadic.cub — the count of negative directions at fixed precision. The exact elimination of chem/witness.cub
-- works on integers that grow with the size of the matrix (its cost for n rows of b bits goes as n⁵b²); here every
-- number keeps the precision chosen, and the cost goes as n³. Statement checked (`dy-cert`): given an integer
-- symmetric matrix A, a radius ρ, a scale t, a unit lower-triangular L (integers, diagonal t, so K = L/t) and a
-- diagonal d (integers), every symmetric matrix within ρ of A (in the operator norm) has exactly c negative
-- eigenvalues and none zero, c the number of negative d_i. Why: R = t²A − L·d·Lᵀ is computed exactly, so
-- A = K·d·Kᵀ + R/t²; a symmetric matrix's norm is at most its largest absolute row sum r, so the matrix in question
-- is K·d·Kᵀ + E with ‖E‖ ≤ ρ + r/t²; K·d·Kᵀ has the signs of d (Sylvester) and no eigenvalue smaller in size than
-- min|d|/‖K⁻¹‖²; for unit lower-triangular K = 1 + N the entries of K⁻¹ are bounded by those of (1 − |N|)⁻¹, whose
-- row sums w solve w_i = 1 + Σ_{j<i} |N_ij| w_j (`dy-fw`, rounded up), and likewise the column sums u, so
-- ‖K⁻¹‖² ≤ max w · max u; eigenvalues move by at most ‖E‖ (Weyl). So the check is
--   min|d|·t² > max w · max u · (t²ρ + r),
-- additions, multiplications and comparisons only. L and d come from an elimination in fixed point (`dy-elim`),
-- outside the statement's trust: any L and d that pass are a proof. A rational matrix is rounded entry by entry to
-- scale 2^k (`dy-round`), which moves it by less than its number of rows, and that is the ρ (`dy-count`).
-- Taken here, not proved: Sylvester's law for K·d·Kᵀ (chem/sylvester.cub proves it for the forms used there), the
-- bound of a symmetric matrix's norm by its row sums, the bound on K⁻¹, and Weyl's inequality.
import "witness.cub"
import "linalg.cub"

